* Sat Oct 17 2026 Sebastian Wolf <sebastian@ygriega.de> 0.3-1
- Records runs, walks, bike rides, rowing and drives with GPS: duration,
  distance, current and average pace and speed, elevation and climb, with the
  position and the route on an OpenStreetMap map. Pause with a tap, stop by holding the button. Each
  activity is stored as a GPX file and listed on the main page, newest first,
  where it can be replayed with a slider. The steps move to the lower third of
  the main page.
- GPS positions, speed and altitude go through a smoothing filter tuned to the
  activity type before they are shown or stored: it weighs each fix by its
  accuracy, leaves out jumps and spikes, and keeps the route, the distance,
  the pace and the climb steady.
- Swiping the values aside shows charts of the pace and the elevation over
  the activity's time, with the average pace as a dotted line; two dots
  below say which is shown.
- Deletes an activity from its context menu in the list or from the pulley
  menu of its page, after a ten-second countdown that a tap undoes.
- While an activity is recorded, the cover shows its type, duration, distance
  and pace, and a cover action pauses and resumes it.
- Auto-pause, switched beside the Pause button and on by default: the
  recording pauses on its own when standing still, or when moving too fast
  for the activity type, as on a bus, and resumes once moving on; walking and
  running, steps tell as well. The time paused is left out from the moment
  the standing began. Half an hour paused, the activity is stopped and stored.
  Half a minute without a usable GPS position, the page says so.
- Swiped to after the charts, how far the activity went through forest,
  farmland, meadows and parks, built-up land, water and the like, in
  kilometres and per cent. It is read off the map's tiles - only those shown
  on screen, during the activity or after it - and kept in the GPX file.
- Map tiles are downloaded only while the map is on screen, as
  OpenStreetMap's tile usage policy asks, no longer with the display off.
- Asks for the Location and Internet permissions; no longer for Sensors, which
  Location includes.

* Wed Oct 14 2026 Sebastian Wolf <sebastian@ygriega.de> 0.2-1
- Keeps the steps of each day in a local SQLite database, so the count survives
  closing the app. The main page and the cover show today's steps, the main
  page also those of the three days before. Rhythm-aware step detection: fewer
  double counts and misses, and slow walks are counted.

* Tue Oct 13 2026 Sebastian Wolf <sebastian@ygriega.de> 0.1-1
- First version: counts steps with the accelerometer from the moment the app
  starts, also while the display is off, and shows the count on the main page
  and on the cover. Nothing is stored yet; closing the app ends the count.
